As an inexpensive, commercially available multi-dentate ligand, camphoric acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cam}\right)^{25}$ is a very good choice to explore the chiral coordination chemistry, which is comparable to the naturally occurring amino acids. $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ is a cyclopentane derivative containing two carboxylic acid functional groups on the 1 and 3-carbon atoms. The 1-carbon atom has an additional methyl group and two methyl substituents are present on the 2-carbon atom, thereby making $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ an enantiopure ditopic organic linker with rich and colorful coordination modes. Moreover, the coordination chemistry of D-cam specifically gives rise to many interesting chiral features applicable to both materials and life sciences, such as asymmetrical synthesis or crystallization, homochiral structural design, chiral induction, absolute helical control and ligand handedness. To the best of our knowledge, D-cam represents a very unique example which includes almost all of the chiral phenomena known in coordination chemistry. The detailed chiral chemistry and applications of metal–camphorate frameworks are introduced in this review.
## 2. Synthetic strategies for metal– camphorate frameworks
Chemical synthesis of MOFs is largely dependent on the use of solvents, and the well-known synthetic methods include hydrothermal, solvothermal and ionothermal synthesis.26–29 Recently, a new method named urothermal synthesis has also emerged.30,31 Such typical synthetic methods have been widely used for preparing metal–camphorate framework materials. For each synthetic approach, several interesting points are worthy of mention and listed below.
The first exploration on homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks was achieved by hydrothermal synthesis. Chen et al.32 reported a series of isostructural compounds $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{cam})_{2}\cdot$ $\left(1,4\mathrm{-dimb}\right)]_{n}\left[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{H}}$ , $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ , $\mathrm{Co_{1/2}^{I I}N i_{1/2}^{I I}}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{1/4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3/4}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{1/3}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Ni}_{2/3}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , and $\mathrm{Co}_{2/3}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{Ni}_{1/3}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ; 1,4-dimb $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,4-di-(1-imidazolyl-methyl)benzene] with mixed ligands and different ratios of metal ions.32 Under the hydrothermal conditions, the changeable metal ion ratio led to tunable magnetic properties for chiral molecular magnets based on enantiopure $\mathbf{\omega}_{\mathrm{D-H_{2}c a m}}$ .
For every synthetic procedure, variable experimental parameters (such as $\mathsf{p H}$ , solvent, temperature and charge) need to be investigated. Zhang et al. first observed the temperature dependent charge distribution in three homochiral cadmium camphorate frameworks, two of them being prepared from hydrothermal reactions.33 These camphorates exhibit various degrees of charge separation associated with the degree of hydration and are controllable by temperature. The most hydrated phase $[\mathbf{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Cam})_{2}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{3}]$ has a charge distribution comparable to microporous aluminophosphates whereas the anhydrous homochiral framework $[\mathbf{Cd}_{2}(\mathbf{D}\cdot\mathbf{cam})_{2}]_{n}$ has a charge distribution comparable to silicalites (Fig. 1). This work revealed that charge separation is favored at low temperature (room temperature in this case) and becomes progressively smaller when the degree of hydration is decreased (as a result of the increasing reaction temperature).
Hydrothermal in situ ligand synthesis is a very active area of research for developing new organic reactions and new functional MOF materials.34 During the metal–camphorate framework assembly, we also occasionally found unusual in situ organic reactions. For example, an interesting tetradentate ligand, 1,2,4,5-tetra(4-pyridyl)benzene (TPB) was in situ synthesized via the dehydrogenative coupling of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -trimethylenedipyridine (TMDPy) under hydrothermal conditions, generating a new
