It is obvious that the formation of TPB from two TMDPy molecules is unlikely to occur by conventional organic reactions as the simultaneous activation of six inert $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ bonds would require considerable activation energy. The addition of $\mathbf{GeO}_{2}$ (or $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{BO}_{3}\mathbf{.}$ ) is essential for the coupling reaction even though it is not incorporated into the final crystal structure. The facile synthesis of such a unique tetradentate TPB dye may allow its use as a 4-connected molecular building block for the construction of homochiral open-framework materials. In the compound $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{TPB})]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ the square-planar TPB ligand functions as a tetradentate ligand connecting four $\mathrm{Zn^{II}}$ ions through its four peripheral pyridyl groups, leading to the formation of a 2D $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TPB})]_{n}$ sheet. Adjacent parallel sheets are pillared by the D-cam ligands, thereby generating a 3D homochiral framework based on 1D chiral connectivity and 2D achiral connectivity (Fig. 2). Such unusual construction of a homochiral framework based on tetrahedral and square-planer nodes represents a new synthetic pathway for the synthesis of 4-connected homochiral materials.
The in situ ligand formation plays a unique role in the synthesis of MOF materials, however, for a given metal–ligand system, in situ ligand formation can be limiting, especially for the synthesis of kinetically stable porous phases, because reaction conditions must be chosen to meet the requirement of both in situ ligand formation and MOF crystal growth. For homochiral MOFs, less harsh reaction conditions are also desirable because of the reduced risk of ligand racemization. Bu et al. proposed and demonstrated the concept that the use of a pre-synthesized ligand (in place of an in situ formed ligand) to decouple the ligand synthesis reaction from MOF crystal growth allows access to more porous and kinetically stable phases that may not be possible through the in situ process. A homochiral Zn-D-camphorate-TPB system is chosen to illustrate this concept. A new MCamF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{TPB})_{0.5}]$ had been synthesized that contains infinite homochiral sheets of zinc paddlewheel dimers and D-camphorate ligands.36 An exceptional feature is the novel X-pillaring mechanism, in distinct contrast with simple pillaring by rod-like ditopic ligands (here called the I-pillaring mechanism). The perfect geometry matching in both pillaring distances and angles between TPB X-pillars and wavy homochiral sheets allows the TPB ligand to act as X-shaped pillars that prop up homochiral layers with two pyridyl groups on each side, leading to a highly open and rigid 3-D homochiral porous framework.
Another interesting example concerns the hydrothermal in situ synthesis of an enantiopure ligand.18,37 As a derivative of camphoric acid, (S)-camphanic acid, which contains an ester group and a carboxylate group, can be used as the precursor for the synthesis of an enantiopure dicarboxylate, 1,2,2-trimethylcyclopent-3-ene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid (denoted as tced here). Meanwhile, two 3-D flexible homochiral 4-connected PtS-type framework structures, $\mathrm{Zn(tced)}$ and $\mathrm{Zn_{4}(t c e d)_{4}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy)}}$ $(4,4^{\prime}\cdot$ 一 bpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) were obtained, in which the metal cation mediates the chiral ligand transformation of (S)-camphanic acid to form a new enantiopure unsaturated dicarboxylate as a 4-connected unit (Fig. 3).38 The new enantiopure ligand prepared here may have useful applications in enantioselective processes, such as cycloaddition involving this double bond. Furthermore, this unsaturated enantiopure dicarboxylate may be immobilized onto a silica gel substrate through coupling between the functional double bond and Si–OH groups. The resulting materials may have potential applications as a stationary phase for chiral separation.
Solvothermal synthesis of metal camphorate frameworks has also been widely investigated. One typical example is to use different solvents to control distinct homochiral metal camphorate architectures. Four homochiral framework materials that exhibit homochiral connectivity ranging from 1-,2- to 3-dimensions, namely, $[\bf M\bar{n}_{3}(H C O O)_{4}(\mathrm{{D}\mathrm{{-}\mathrm{{cam})}}]_{n},}$ $[\bf M g_{3}(\bf H C O O)_{4}.$ ${\big(}{\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}\mathbf{cam}{\big)}{\big]}_{n},$ , $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-c}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{m})_{2}(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{F})_{2}]_{n}$ and $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}(\mathrm{p-cam})_{2}]_{n},$ were synthesized by employing different solvents, highlighting the versatility of the solvothermal synthetic system.39 MOFs $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{O})_{4}(\mathbf{p}\mathbf{-c}\mathbf{am})]_{n}$ and $[\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{4}(\mathrm{p-cam})]_{n}$ are allomers in which homochiral features serve as decorations in 3-D metal–oxygen $\left(3{\cdot}\mathbf{D}\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}{-}\mathbf{O}\right.$ or $\mathbf{Mg-O}]$ frameworks. The most interesting feature here is that the 1-D homochiral chains can be considered not only as a part of the 3-D framework, but also as decorative ligands to further strengthen the existing 3-D metal–oxygen framework. The MOF $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{m})_{2}.}\end{array}$ $\big(\mathrm{DMF}\big)_{2}\big]_{n}$ shows a homochiral 2-D layered structure with dinuclear Mn units bridged by the D-cam ligands (Fig. 4). The MOF $[\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{4}(\mathrm{p-cam})]_{n}$ displays a $\mathbf{3-D}\mathbf{C}^{3}\mathbf{A}^{0}$ type framework with $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ ions and D-cam ligands and belongs to the PtS net by considering $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ as a tetrahedral node and D-cam as a planar 4-connected node (Fig. 4). The dimensions of homochiral connectivity may be equal to or lower than the overall framework dimensions because of the additional crosslinking by achiral ligands.
Ionothermal synthesis of homochiral MOFs was first developed by Morris et al.40 They used a chiral ionic liquid containing L-aspartate to induce homochirality in the MOF material constructed from entirely achiral building blocks. In addition to the solvent properties, cationic and anionic parts of the ionic liquids can influence the synthetic process through direct incorporation (either individually or in combination) into crystal structures. Zhang et al. first employed enantiopure D-camphoric acid $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}\right)$ , as well as achiral $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -oxybis(benzoic acid) $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{ob}\mathbf{b}\right)$ in two different ionic liquids for the ionothermal synthesis, which resulted in a number of open-framework materials that contain eight-coordinated $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ sites as the tetrahedral node.41 They further demonstrated the unusual triple roles of ionic liquids in these anionic low-connectivity frameworks (ALFs): (1) solvent and cationic structure-directing agents, (2) solvent only, and (3) solvent and cationic/anionic structure-directing agents. In all ALFs, the negative framework charge is balanced by extra-framework organic cations (tetraalkylammonium cations and imidazolium cations), and each 8-coordinate $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ site is bonded to four carboxylate ligands (similar to $\mathrm{Si^{4+}}$ or ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ in zeolites). Thus, this synthetic method goes beyond the traditional limitation that relies on 4-coordinated elements (e.g. $\mathrm{Si^{4+}}$ and $\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ ) to create 4- and 3-connected frameworks (Fig. 5). That makes it possible to create 3-D low-connectivity (3- or 4-connected) and low-density (as low as 0.73 tetrahedral nodes per $\mathbf{nm}^{3}$ ) frameworks from high-coordination elements (coordination number 8). The three different functions of the ionic liquid further extend the applicability of this method and make it possible to create a diversity of homochiral framework materials with various compositions and topologies.
In addition to the traditional synthetic methods above, urothermal synthesis, another versatile method based on the use of various urea derivatives as solvents, was explored for the metal camphorate assembly. A particularly interesting finding is the reaction of chiral D-camphoric acid with ethyleneurea to generate a new enantiopure ligand (ttipo) found in $[\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}(t t i p o)_{4}}]$ (also called URO-502, ttipo $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathbf{\Lambda}6{,}7{,}7$ -trimethyl2,3,7,8-tetrahydroimidazo[1,2-a]pyridin-5(6H)-one) (Fig. 6).30 It demonstrated that the versatility of the urothermal method extends beyond the synthesis of crystalline porous materials to include unusual in situ organic reactions. It is expected that further refinement of the urothermal method will have great potential for the creation of novel homochiral porous materials with promising applications by introducing enantiopure ligands.
## 3. Structural diversity of metal–camphorate frameworks
In addition to the need to devise new synthetic procedures for preparing novel homochiral materials, there is also a pressing need for a general classification scheme that can take into account the complex and diverse structural features of crystalline homochiral materials. There are more than one hundred metal–camphorate frameworks, so a rational classification system could greatly improve the understanding of their structures and potential properties. Recently, Cheetham et al. proposed a very useful scheme for the classification of hybrid organic–inorganic frameworks.42 The symbol $\mathbf{I}^{n}\mathbf{O}^{m}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{I}}=$ inorganic and ${\bf O}={\bf\partial}$ organic, $n=0{-}3$ and $m=0{-}3$ ) was used to designate the dimensionality of inorganic and organic sub-structures. For example, a structure in which a two-dimensional $\mathrm{znso}_{3}$ sheet is pillared by ethylenediamine is considered two-dimensional with respect to the inorganic connectivity and one-dimensional with respect to the organic connectivity.43 Such structure is therefore designated as $\mathbf{I}^{2}\mathbf{O}^{1}$ . Overall it is a covalent three-dimensional $(2\ +\ 1)$ open framework. The $\mathbf{I}^{n}\mathbf{O}^{m}$ classification scheme allows a total of 16 structural categories based on the values of $n$ and $m$ . The most intriguing aspect is its suggestion of 4-,5-, and 6-dimensional structures (for $n+m>3$ , which at the time of its proposal were not known.
While the $\mathbf{I}^{n}\mathbf{O}^{m}$ scheme is useful for analyzing the functionality of inorganic and organic substructures, it does not address the unique features of homochiral organic–inorganic hybrids.
We propose the following classification scheme for homochiral materials. In a homochiral material, the connectivity between metal sites and enantiopure ligands can range from 0 to 3 (denoted $\mathbf{C}^{n}$ ) and so is the connectivity between metal sites and achiral ligands (denoted $\mathbf{A}^{m}$ , if present). Therefore, $\mathbf{C}^{n}\mathbf{A}^{m}$ $\stackrel{\cdot}{n}=0{-}3$ and $m\:=\:0{-}3$ ) can be used to classify different homochiral structures into 16 different categories (Table 1). Again, the possibility for 4-,5- and 6-dimensional structures suggested by this scheme is intriguing. The use of 4-,5-, and 6-dimensionality to describe a hybrid structure as provided by the $\mathbf{I}^{n}\mathbf{O}^{m}$ and $\mathbf{C}^{n}\mathbf{A}^{m}$ schemes is a valuable method that provides insight into the fundamental structural principles and also guidelines for the synthetic design of novel materials. The structural features highlighted by such schemes can be correlated with physical and chemical properties such as thermal and chemical stability, magnetism, and enantioselectivity. For example, a $\mathbf{C}^{n}\mathbf{A}^{m}$ structure with a large $n$ may provide a strong homochiral environment that serves to enhance chiral recognition and enantioselectivity.
For the construction of metal–camphorate frameworks, three design strategies have been applied: (i) simple metal camphorate connectivity $(\mathbf{C}^{n}\mathbf{A}^{0})$ where camphorate is a bridging linker; (ii) metal camphorates with auxiliary ligands $(\mathbf{C}^{n}\mathbf{A}^{m})$ where camphorate can be a $\upmu_{2}$ -linker or a dangling part; and (iii) camphorate templated frameworks $\left(\mathbf{C}^{0}\mathbf{A}^{m}\right)$ where camphorate is only a guest molecule. The reported metal–camphorate frameworks are listed in Table 2 and include the dimensionality of substructures with respect to metal–chiral ligand connectivity $(\mathbf{C}^{n})$ and metal–achiral ligand connectivity $\left(\mathbf{A}^{m}\right)$ .
## 3.1 Homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks ranging from 1-,2- to 3-dimensions
In the design and synthesis of functional homochiral MOFs, one of the most effective and direct approaches is to use enantiopure chiral ligands as starting materials. In known 3-D (3-dimension) homochiral MOFs, enantiopure ligands generally serve as structural building units to crosslink inorganic units into covalent frameworks, and chiral ligands are essential for the extended 3-D framework connectivity.
Another case in point is the reaction of $(+)-(1R,3S)$ -camphoric acid with triorganotin(IV) chloride in the presence of sodium ethoxide which results in four new chiral triorganotin(IV) MOFs.76 Except for the MOF $\mathrm{\Gamma(R_{3}S n)_{2}[C_{8}H_{14}(C O O)_{2}](R=P h C H_{2})};$ all of the complexes were characterized by X-ray crystallography. The MOF $(\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{Sn})_{2}[\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{14}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}](\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5})$ shows a dinuclear structure, and the MOF $\{(\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{Sn})_{2}[\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{14}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}]\}_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{\tilde{R}}=n$ -butyl) has a 1D zigzag chain structure and further forms the final 3D organotin framework through weak intermolecular $_{\mathrm{~\tiny~\cdot~}\cdot\mathrm{sn}}$ interactions. The MOF $\{(\mathrm{R}_{3}\mathrm{Sn})_{2}[\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{14}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}]\}_{n}$ ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl) displays a 2D layer structure in which $(+)$ -camphoric acid act as tetradentate ligands coordinated to trimethyltin(IV) ions. The different structures may be related to the steric hindrance of the triorganotin group.
Similarly, a nanosized chiral octanuclear cage $[(\mathrm{UO}_{2})_{8}-$ $\left(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}\right)_{12}\mathrm{H}_{8}\right]_{3}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ which displays an approximately cubic shape with the metal ions as apexes and an internal cavity of ca. $7\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ in length.78 $[\mathrm{(UO_{2})_{8}(D\mathrm{\overline{{\cdot}c a m})_{12}H_{8}}}]_{3}$ is the first example of a homoleptic nanosized cage including uranyl ions and can be further utilized as a supramolecular SBU to generate coordination polymers, exemplified by the compound $\mathrm{\big[Ba\big(H}_{2}O\big)_{8}\big]}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{\small{[(UO_{2})_{8}B a_{2}(p\mathrm{-cam)_{12}(H_{2}O)_{4}]\cdot8H_{2}O_{5}}}}$ obtained in the presence of $\mathbf{Ba(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ . In this structure, which retains the overall packing mode of cages shown in $[(\mathbf{UO}_{2})_{8}(\mathbf{L}_{12}\mathbf{H}_{8})]_{3}{\cdot}12\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ a two-dimensional assembly is formed through bridging by the carboxylate-bound alkaline-earth metal cations. However, whereas $\left[\mathrm{(UO_{2})_{8}}\mathrm{(D\mathrm{-cam)_{12}H_{8}}}\right]_{3}\cdot12\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ displays extended channels, these are obstructed by counter ions in $\mathrm{\big[Ba(H_{2}O)_{8}\big]_{2}[(U O_{2})_{8}B a_{2}(p\mathrm{-cam)_{12}\mathrm{-}}}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)_{4}\mathbf{J}{\cdot}8\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ . This $\mathbf{Ba-U}$ compound has built in chirality, which was not the case of the former examples.
Some of them resemble secondary building units (SBUs), which are also in MOFs constructed by $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ . $\mathbf{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ reacts with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ in acetonitrile to immediately form the soluble, unsymmetrically dimetallic $[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{Hcam})_{3}]^{+}$ cations. These dimers have 3-fold paddle-wheels, which associate together through the neutral acid units to build the MOF $[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{Hcam})_{3}{\cdot}3\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}]{\cdot}\mathbf{NO}_{3}$ MeCN.44
The network consists of a series of fused $\mathbf{Mg}_{12}$ cages that include 12 water molecules at their centers, creating isolated 0D cavities within the structure (Fig. 7). Overall, the extended structure of $[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{Hcam})_{3}{\cdot}3\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}]{\cdot}\mathbf{NO}_{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{MeCN}$ is a body-centered cubic (bcu) lattice, with the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{12}$ cages being used as eightconnected nodes. The chirality of the $(1R,3S)–(+)$ -camphoric acid is retained, leading to crystallization in the very rare chiral, cubic space group $I23$ . Mass spectrometry studies of the precursor acetonitrile solutions of $[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{Hcam})_{3}{\cdot}3\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}]{\cdot}\mathbf{NO}_{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{MeCN}$ show the stable SBU $[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{Hcam})_{3}]^{+}$ cation prior to crystal growth and lends strong support to the assertion that it is the true building block for the network assembly.
Two thermally stable homochiral MOFs, $\left[\mathrm{Co}\left(\mathrm{D-cam}\right)\right]$ and $[\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathbf{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathbf{-H}_{2}\mathbf{cam})_{2}]$ , using $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ and the corresponding metal salts were explored by hydro(solvo)thermal synthesis.49 In the MOF $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{n-c a m})];$ the two crystallographically distinct ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions show a five coordination of $\mathbf{CoO}_{5}$ , leading to the formation of one-dimensional cobalt–carboxylate chains. As displayed in Fig. 8, the ${\bf C o(O_{2}C)}_{2}$ chains are aligned parallel to the crystallographic [100] direction, with the syn–syn bidentate-bridging and bidentate-chelating-bridging carboxylate groups alternating on either side of the chain backbone. Each Co–carboxylate chain is cross-linked to four neighboring chains by enantiopure D-cam ligands into a 3-D-homochiral framework.
In the MOF $[\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{c}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{m})_{2}].$ , the two crystallographically distinct ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centers together with four syn–syn bidentatebridging carboxylate groups from four D-cam ligands form a dinuclear paddlewheel $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ building unit with a metal– metal separation of 2.5585(13) $\mathring\mathbf{A}.$ . These binuclear Cu units are cross-linked into infinite planar homochiral layers of $\left\{\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{cam}_{2}\right\}$ via enantiopure D-cam linkages, having grid-like (4,4)-topology. In these two MOFs, the chirality of the enantiopure organic molecule $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ was successfully introduced into the metal–organic hybrid networks.
Two series of metal–camphorate frameworks $[\mathrm{Ln}\big(\mathrm{p-cam}\big)\cdot$ $\left(\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{COO}\right)\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)]$ and $[\mathrm{Ln(p\mathrm{-}c a m)(H C O O)}]$ were synthesized with acetate (early lanthanides La–Dy) or formate (late lanthanides
Tb–Lu and Y) as the auxiliary ligand, respectively.79 Bu et al. found that larger early Ln ions only crystallize with the larger acetate ligands (as well as D-camphorate) into a MOF structure denoted as type 1, while smaller late Ln ions only crystallize with the smaller formate ligands into a MOF structure denoted as type 2 (see Fig. 8). One notable difference between type 1 and type 2 is that $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions have different coordination numbers. Each $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ in type 1 has one pendant $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , making it 8-coordinated. In contrast, without $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , each $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ in type 2 is only 7-coordinated (Fig. 8a and d). This difference in the coordination number correlates with the ionic radii of the smaller ions adopting a lower coordination number. Both structures can be understood as 3D frameworks assembled from parallel inorganic chains with camphorate as the bridging ligands. The most notable one is that two adjacent $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are bridged by two –COO groups from two camphorate ligands and a monodentate ligand, which is acetate for type 1 and formate for type 2 (Fig. 8b and e). This monodentate carboxylate is always chelating to one metal ion and at the same time with one of its oxygens bridging to the other metal in a $\upmu_{2}{\bf-O}$ manner. Thus, an $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}$ infinite chain is formed by the monodentate ligand. Besides their roles in reinforcing the connection of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions already linked together by D-cam ligands, they also serve to render the overall framework neutral. This result demonstrated that crystallization processes involving $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are very sensitive to ionic radii and that the difference in the ionic radius between two $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions dictates the unequal concentrations of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ in Ln-MOF crystals.
## 3.2 Homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks with neutral auxiliary achiral ligands
In the synthesis of homochiral MOFs, it is possible to create 3D homochiral structures by using only enantiopure ligands as crosslinkers just as described in reported literature studies,16,41,44,49,50,83 however, it is often advantageous to apply a multi-ligand synthetic strategy because the use of two functionally different ligands provides an additional level of control in the framework structure and charge density. Most experiments have demonstrated that a predominant structural mode resulting from the use of such a dual-ligand method is the formation of neutral metal–carboxylate substructures (e.g. chains or sheets) that are subsequently linked or pillared by auxiliary neutral bipyridine ligands into 3D homochiral frameworks.64,86 One widely used dual-ligand synthetic strategy in crystal engineering is to combine the bi- or polydentate carboxylate ligand with the diimine ligand.64,87,88
Using this strategy, four new homochiral and one racemic chiral 3-D materials assembled from transition metals $({\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ), $\mathbf{D}^{-}$ or DL- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cam}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -trimethylenedipyridine (TMDPy) were prepared.6 $^3\mathrm{\DeltaNi(\mathrm{D\cdotCam})(\mathrm{TMDPy})}$ features an unusual homochiral three-dimensional supramolecular assembly resulting from catenation of 4-connected bilayers, while $\mathrm{\mathbf{Co}(\mathrm{{D}\mathrm{{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{{cam})}(\mathrm{{TMDPy})}}}}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a homochiral 3-fold interpenetrating diamond net. In comparison, $\mathrm{Fe_{2}^{I I}\left(D\mathrm{\cdot}c a m\right)_{2}(T M D P y)}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\big(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{TMDPy}\big).$ , and $\mathrm{CO_{2}(D L\mathrm{-}C a m)_{2}(T M D P y)}$ show a non-interpenetrating pcu (primitive cubic) net. Such three distinct structural architectures that contain unusual structural motifs are closely associated with the versatile conformations of the auxiliary TMDPy ligand (Fig. 9). It is worth noting that the three homochiral open-framework architectures contain redox-active metal centers capable of providing multifunctional materials that integrate enantioselective catalysis with redox catalysis.
The diversity of architectures also exists for five new cadmium(II) chiral networks with enantiopure D-camphoric acid and different spacer lengths of flexible bis(triazole) ligands under hydrothermal conditions.73 Complex $\left[\{{\bf C d}(\mathrm{n}\mathrm{-cam})(\mathrm{bte})\}{\bf\cdot H}_{2}{\bf O}\right]_{n}$ (bte $^{=1,2}$ -bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)-ethane) presents a 2D rectangular network with (4,4) topology. Complex $\left[\mathrm{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam})_{4}(\mathrm{btp})_{2}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}\right]_{n}$ (btp $\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{1},\hat{\mathbf{\Psi}}$ -bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)propane) displays a chiral 3-D framework with $(3^{2}{\cdot}4)(3{\cdot}8^{4}{\cdot}12)(8^{3})(4{\cdot}8{\cdot}10)$ topology. The compound $\operatorname{\left[\left\{Cd(n\mathrm{-}c a m)(b t b)_{1.5}\right\}\cdot3H_{2}O\right]}_{n}$ (btb $^{=1,4}$ -bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)butane) has a 2D honeycomb motif with one-dimensional (1D) nanosized channels. The compound $\left[\{\mathrm{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-cam})_{4}(\mathrm{bth})_{4}\}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right]_{n}$ ${\mathrm{bth}}=1,6-$ bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)-hexane) is a 2-D parallelogram sheet with (4,4) topology. The compound $[\{{\bf C d}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hcam})_{2}(\mathrm{bth})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]_{n}$ shows a chiral 1D fish-bone chain which is further linked by hydrogen-bonding interactions to form the final 3-D supramolecular framework. The multifarious architectures of these compounds mainly result from the bis(triazole) ligands with different spacer lengths and conformational flexibility.
The synthetic strategy involving a dual-ligand has been widely used in the synthesis of MOF materials.89–93 Generally, the auxiliary neutral ligands pillar low dimensional chiral substructures including enantiopure ligands into higher dimensional frameworks. In the synthesis of MOFs, it is worth noting that the crystallization of chiral or achiral camphorate-based MOFs can be controlled by the configurational rigidity of N-donor co-ligands. If the flexible N-donor co-ligands adopt asymmetrical conformation such as T, TG, or TTTGT, then the resultant camphorate-based MOFs are chiral. By contrast, when the flexible N-donor co-ligands take symmetrical conformation such as GTG or GTTG, then the resultant camphorate based complexes are achiral.58 In the chiral compounds of $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(n\mathrm{-cam)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy)}}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-}H c a m)_{2}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-}b p y)\cdot H_{2}O_{2}}$ the somewhat flexible N-donor $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy co-ligand is badly distorted with the dihedral angle of ca. $20.5^{\circ}$ and $1.1^{\circ}$ respectively between two pyridine rings and belongs to the $C_{2}$ point group without plane symmetry. However, for the achiral compounds of $\mathrm{(Zn_{3}(D\mathrm{\cdot}c a m)_{2}(B t a)_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\big(\mathrm{DL}\mathrm{-cam}\big)_{2}\cdot$ $\mathbf{(Bta)}_{2}$ ,58 the completely rigid N-donor Bta co-ligand does not show any distortion and belongs to the $C_{2\mathbf{v}}$ point group with plane symmetry. To some extent, these research results demonstrate that the configurational rigidity of the co-ligand can be used to control the crystallization of chiral or achiral camphorate-based complexes.
Apart from the generation of a diversity of architectures with auxiliary ligands of different lengths, alteration of the molar ratios may also form various frameworks. Two cadmium(II) homochiral MOFs involving chiral camphorate ligands, formulated as $\left[\mathrm{Cd_{2}(n\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}(d i p)_{2}}\right]\cdot(\mathrm{H_{2}O)_{6},[\mathrm{Cd_{2}(\mathrm{n\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}(d i p)(H_{2}O)}](d i p=0)}}$ 3,8-diimidazol-1,10-phen),69 were synthesized by using different molar ratios of starting materials (Fig. 10). $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dip})_{2}]$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{6}$ is a homochiral 2D layer, while $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ has a homochiral 3D structure with $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}/\mathrm{C}\mathrm{d}(\mathrm{II})/\mathrm{D}{\cdot}0$ am. In these two MOFs, it was suggested that the different molar ratios of starting materials in the process of metal–ion complexation contribute to the formation of different networks.
In building chiral frameworks, the structural properties of the metal center such as geometry, ionic radii, steric effects, and M–L–M angles (e.g., Si–O–Al angle in zeolites) are contributing factors that should be considered.94–98 In $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ described above, replacing a Cd salt with a Zn salt led to $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(D\mathrm{-cam)_{2}(d i p)]\cdot(H_{2}O)_{5}}}}$ which has a quite different structure. In the two MOFs, the different radii of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ cations lead to different coordination geometry, as well as different framework construction.
## 3.3 Camphorate templated homochiral frameworks
Except using enantiopure ligands as structural building units, one of the most effective strategies now known for increasing the diversity of microporous materials is the use of charged organic templates (also called structure-directing agents, most commonly tetraalkylammonium cations or protonated cationic amines) to direct the self-assembly of molecular precursors (almost always achiral) into extended frameworks.99,100 Obviously, a synthetic approach that allows the self-assembly of enantiopure chiral molecular precursors into extended frameworks templated by charged organic templates will help to greatly expand the diversity of homochiral MOFs, similar to the impact of charged organic molecules on the synthesis of aluminosilicates, zeolites, microporous phosphates, and many other inorganic open-framework materials.101–104
This strategy has led to the synthesis of new camphorate templated homochiral open frameworks. For example, the use of such organically templated synthesis led to materials with three distinct topological types: The MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})_{4}]$ $\left(\mathrm{p{-}H c a m}\right)_{2}{\cdot}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shows a 2-fold interpenetrating diamond net templated by an enantiopure-anion (D-Hcam) (Fig. 10).60 MOFs $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{4}(O H)(p\mathrm{-cam)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}}.$ Hdmadma, $\mathrm{[Cd_{4}(O H)}.$ $\mathrm{\big(D{\cdot}c a m\big)_{4}(p y)\big]{\cdot}H d m a}$ , and $[\mathrm{Co_{4}(O H)(p\mathrm{-cam)_{4}(p y)}}]$ Hdma display a homochiral body-centered cubic (bcu) net based on unusual 8-connected square-planar $\left(\mathbf{M}_{4}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H}\right)^{7+}\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{d}^{2+},\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{2+}\right)$ units. The MOF $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{2}(D\mathrm{-}c a m)_{3}]\cdot(H d m a)_{2}\cdot(d m a)_{4}\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ exhibits a previously unknown homochiral 6-connected net. It is interesting that the microporosity of the MOF $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{4}(O H)(p\mathrm{-cam)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}},$ Hdmadma is demonstrated by the solvent-exchange-induced single-crystal to single-crystal transformation. By immersion of $\mathrm{[Cd_{4}(O H)\cdot}$ $\left(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}\right)_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ Hdma dma in a solvent mixture of DMF/ $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{5}\mathbf{OH}_{I}$ /pyridine at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 8 hours, the solvent pyridine molecule exchanged the coordinated water molecule in $\mathrm{[Cd_{4}(O H)}.$ $\big(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}\big)_{4}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}\big].$ Hdmadma to give the compound $\mathrm{[Cd_{4}(O H)\cdot}$ $\left(\mathrm{{D}{\cdot}c a m)_{4}(p y)}\right]$ Hdma as confirmed by single crystal X-ray diffraction (Fig. 11). Clearly, this work demonstrates that the versatile zeolite synthetic approach (i.e., templating by charged organic structure-directing agents) can be extended to direct the selfassembly of enantiopure molecular precursors into 3-D homochiral metal–organic frameworks.
Architectural control of molecular self-organization is of importance for the development of functional materials. Charged organic templates can be used as the structure-directing agents as presented above. Recently, hydrogen-bonding-directed molecular assembly has attracted much attention in the design of various MOFs by virtue of the directionality and specificity.105 A chiral organometallic crystal architecture of ferrocene bearing the dipeptide chains was achieved by employing D-cam as a hydrogen bonding bridge. It is fascinating that a double-helicallike hydrogen-bonded molecular arrangement was organized using the hydrogen bonding bridge of D-cam.
## 4. Chirality and helicity in metal–camphorate frameworks
Chirality and absolute helicity are intimately associated with the living processes and often closely linked with each other in the same structure even though their origin remains unclear.106–110 For example, enzymes, nucleic acids and other biopolymers made up of L-amino acids or D-sugars, etc. have acquired a definite handedness.111 The research on the relationship between the chirality of molecular building blocks and the helicity of metal– camphorate frameworks could ultimately lead to a better understanding of the origin of asymmetry in homochiral MOF systems.
## 4.1 Absolute helicity induction in homochiral metal– camphorate frameworks
Chirality includes molecular chirality (molecules with chiral structures) and spatial chirality (resulting from crystallizationbased spatial organization of molecular building blocks) whereas helicity is a special form of one-dimensional chirality. Two driving forces have been shown to be able to induce the absolute homo-helicity. One is the internal induction in which the chirality of the molecular building block forms an inherent part of the helical backbone (such as the helicity of DNA based on D-sugars).112 The second one is the external induction in which the chiral source is not part of the helical backbone (such as the formation of homohelical structure upon ligation of a chiral ligand). The control of the absolute helicity is well known in 1-D polymers such as DNA in which the handedness of D-sugars leads to the right-handed helicity. A comparative study of three polymeric isomers in which the chirality of enantiopure ligands dictates the absolute sense of the helix made of achiral components was presented. Three Co(II) isomers assembled from D, or L-, or DL-camphorate together with achiral isonicotinate exhibit a clear relationship between chirality and helicity even though chiral molecules are not in the backbone of the helix: the absolute sense of helix made of achiral components is controlled by chains of metal and enantiopure chiral ligands running perpendicular to the helix.
The most interesting aspect in these structures is the observed correlation between molecular chirality and absolute helicity. A D- or L-cam ligand results in the homohelical arrangement of achiral components: a right-handed helix (P-helix) in $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam})(\mathrm{int})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ (similar to DNA where D-sugars lead to P-helix) and a left-handed helix (M-helix) in $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-cam})(\mathrm{int})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ (Fig. 12).55
4.2 Integrated molecular chirality, absolute helicity, and intrinsic chiral topology in homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks
While chiral materials are common, few are known that integrate molecular chirality, absolute helicity, and 3D intrinsically chiral topological nets in one material. Such multi-homochiral features are highly desirable for enhanced chiral recognition processes that are important for enantioselective catalysis or separation. It is of great interest to explore 3D framework materials that integrate molecular chirality and intrinsic chiral topology because such a study could not only lead to functional framework materials for efficient chiral recognition processes, but also may help to provide fresh opportunities for better understanding the origin of asymmetry in the living system. Zhang and coworkers showed unusual integrated homochirality features in six new 3-D framework materials containing enantiopure building blocks embedded in intrinsically chiral topological nets, of which the quartz net is the most well-known. Each material has three homochiral features: a 3-D intrinsically homochiral net, homo-helicity, and enantiopure molecular chirality (Fig. 13). It is shown that the absolute helicity can be controlled by the chirality of molecular building blocks.
In $\mathrm{\big(TMA\big)}\mathrm{[In(D\mathrm{-cam)}}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{\big(TMA\big)}[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and (Choline) $\mathrm{[In(D\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ $\mathbf{\widetilde{TMA}}=(\mathbf{CH}_{3})_{4}\mathbf{N}^{+})$ , the enantiopure monomers co-exist with the 3-D intrinsically chiral topology of the quartz (denoted qtz) net.50 A striking feature in (TMA)- $\mathrm{[In(n\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{(TMA)[In(L\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ is the helicity induction effect of the enantiopure ligands, resulting in the formation of the homo-helical quartz topology. (TMA)- $\mathrm{\big[In\big(D\mathrm{-}c a m\big)_{2}\big]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{(TMA)[In(L\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ are built from enantiopure $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}^{-}$ or L-camphoric acid, respectively (Fig. 14). In both structures, the absolute helicity is controlled by the handedness of the enantiopure ligand. D-cam leads to the right-handed (P-helix) quartz net in $\mathrm{\big(TMA\big)}[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (denoted $\mathbf{P-D}$ form) whereas L-cam leads to the left-handed (M-helix) quartz net in $\mathrm{(TMA)[In(L\mathrm{-}c a m)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ (denoted $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{L}$ form). The chirality and helicity of the compound $\mathrm{\big(TMA\big)[In\big(D\mathrm{-cam}\big)_{2}]\mathrm{\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ is comparable to DNA, because both adopt the P-D form. In addition to the chiral quartz-type, other intrinsically chiral nets (quartz-dual and srs) have also been made in this work by using D-camphoric acid or L-malic acid. The exploration of such multi-chirality materials and chirality–helicity relationship could lead to new insights into the generation and control of helicity and homochirality in both solidstate materials and biopolymers. The 3D framework materials with integrated homochiral features are expected to have potential in enhanced chiral recognition and enantioselectivity.
4.3 Stretchable helices and chiral and achiral nets with nearly identical unit cells in homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks
Even though recent successes with synthesis of MOFs have opened up new routes towards constructing homochiral MOFs from enantiopure ligands, the homochiral 3D frameworks with the 4-connected topology remain rare. By employing strategies (such as lower temperature and shorter reaction time, or higher temperature and longer reaction), five new homochiral and one achiral 3-D materials with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}^{-}$ or $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}_{\j}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -trimethylenedipyridine (TMDPy) were prepared.65 These materials exhibit three distinct topological features. The flexibility of helices in the structure allows the single-crystal to single-crystal transformation from $\mathrm{Cd(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}c a m)(\mathrm{TMDPy){\cdot}2}H_{2}O}$ to anhydrous $\mathfrak{a}$ -Cd$\mathrm{\big(D\mathrm{-cam}\big)(T M D P y\big)}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , followed by a polymorphic displacive phase transition into $\mathrm{\beta_{\mathrm{-Cd}}(\mathrm{_{D\mathrm{-}C a m})(\mathrm{TMDPy})}}$ at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 15). An interesting discovery is the structure of two homochiral solids, $\mathrm{\beta_{\mathrm{-}C d}(\mathrm{_{D\mathrm{-}C a m})(\mathrm{TMDPy})}}$ and $\mathbf{r}{\mathbf{-Cd}}(\mathbf{\mathrm{D}}{\mathbf{-cam}})(\mathbf{TMDPy})$ , which have the same topological type and yet contain lattice interpenetration of the different order. An unprecedented discovery is that the chirality of the 3D framework can be readily switched between chiral r-Cd(D-cam)(TMDPy) (space group $\left.P2_{1.}\right\rangle$ and achiral $\mathbf{Cd}(\mathrm{DL}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Cam})(\mathbf{TMDPy})$ forms with a negligible change in all sixdimensions of unit cell parameters. It is worth noting that crystals made from enantiopure ligands and racemates generally have very differing crystal structures due to the different symmetry requirements (i.e., forbiddance of inversion axes in homochiral crystals). Homochiral and achiral materials with nearly identical unit cells were found for the first time in this work, providing a general strategy for the direct conversion of an achiral 4-connected net to a homochiral net through judicious ligand design and substitution.
Just as described above, an achiral material with the 4-connected diamond type topology can be readily converted into a homochiral material with a change in the chirality of the basic building block. Obviously, the ability to directly convert an achiral framework into a homochiral framework without any concurrent change in crystal structures or topology offers a convenient path for the rational design of homochiral materials. Zhang and his coworkers prepared three homochiral compounds $([\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{p}{-}\mathrm{cam})_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathbf{bpy})]_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{\mathbf{1}}^{c}}$ $\therefore M=C u;2^{\mathbf{c}}$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ ; $4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}=4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}$ bipyridine; superscript $\cdot_{\mathrm{c}},$ means chiral.) and $[\mathbf{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{D}\mathbf{-cam})_{4}(\mathrm{bpe})_{3}]_{n}$ $(3^{\mathbf{c}}$ , bpe $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ trans-1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)-ethylene) and three racemic chiral compounds $([\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{DL-cam})_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathbf{-bpy})]_{n}$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{1}^{a}}$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u};2^{\mathbf{a}}$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ ; superscript $\mathbf{\dot{a}}_{}^{}$ means achiral.) and $[\mathbf{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{DL}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Cam})_{2}(\mathbf{bpe})_{3/2}]_{n}$ $\left(3^{\mathbf{a}}\right)$ involving either enantiopure (D- $(+).$ -camphoric acid $\scriptstyle\left(=\mathbf{D}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}\right)$ 1 or racemic ligands $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{{DL-}}(+))$ -camphoric acid $\left(=_{\mathrm{DL}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}\right)_{\perp}^{\mathsf{Y}}$ ) by hydrothermal synthesis.64
Compounds $\mathbf{1^{c}}$ and ${\bf1^{a}}$ , despite the fundamental difference in chirality, have nearly identical unit cells and crystal structures which are interesting examples that illustrate the minimal effect of the ligand handedness on the crystallization of chiral and racemic chiral frameworks from enantiopure or racemic ligands (Fig. 16).
When $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used instead of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ the isostructural compounds $2^{\mathbf{c}}$ and $2^{\mathbf{a}}$ with a previously unknown topology were obtained. Such a topological change is primarily due to the different coordination chemistry between $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ (square-pyramidal in $\mathbf{1^{c}}$ and ${\bf1^{a}}$ ) and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ (tetrahedral in $2^{\mathbf{c}}$ and $2^{\mathbf{a}}$ ). $2^{\mathbf{c}}$ and $2^{\mathbf{a}}$ also have nearly identical unit cell parameters between the chiral $2^{\mathbf{c}}$ and racemic chiral $2^{\mathbf{a}}$ forms.
## 4.4 Dual spontaneous resolution in homochiral metal–camphorate frameworks
To better understand the self-assembly process of chiral MOFs, there have also some studies using racemic ligands in the starting materials so that a comparative study between the self-assembly involving enantiopure ligands and that involving racemic ligands can be made. Moreover, the synthesis of homochiral MOFs using enantiopure ligands may also be accompanied by the ligand racemization, which further increases the importance of studying self-assembly processes involving racemic ligands.66,113,114 The configuration of chiral centers in the asymmetric units determines the spatial arrangement through host–guest interaction including hydrogen-bonding or $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. The coordination modes of metal ions or clusters contribute to the different senses of the helices.115–117 The chirality is induced and transferred from the asymmetric units to the helices.
A unique enantioselective process occurred during the solvothermal assembly of $\mathbf{D}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ with $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in triethanolamine $\scriptstyle(=\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathrm{tea})$ , which resulted in two chiral 3-D frameworks, $\mathrm{Zn(A{\cdot}Z n(H t e a))(p{\cdot}c a m)}$ and $\mathrm{Zn(\Delta\mathrm{-Zn(Htea))(\mathrm{L\mathrm{-}C a m).}}}$ These frameworks contain either all D-form or all L-form. It is found that such enantioselection is promoted by an in situ formed racemic chiral metal complex. Triethanolamine acts not only as a solvent, but also as a ligand to generate a racemic building unit via the chelation to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ .66 The chiral recognition between enantiomers of camphorates and chiral zinc complexes results in the enantioselection and spontaneous resolution of both pairs of racemates (Fig. 17). The enantioselective crystallization observed is believed to come from supramolecular interactions during the self-assembly, and is likely controlled by the $a-c$ atom of the camphorate ligand. As illustrated in the figure, the flexible hydrogen bonded helices have to adjust their handedness to effectively accommodate the $\mathfrak{a}$ -C atom of the camphorate ligand. The chiral recognition between hydrogen bonded helices and camphorate ligands also contributes to the final cooperative double spontaneous resolutions. Of particular interest is the cooperative double spontaneous resolution of two pairs of distinctly different racemates: the $\Lambda\Delta{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{{Zn}(\mathrm{{Htea})}}$ units and the DL-cam ligands (Fig. 18). It is demonstrated that this self-assembly process for the formation of a chiral framework entails cooperative enantioselectivity $(\Lambda{\cdot}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Htea})\leftrightarrow\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{cam}$ and $\Delta{\cdot}\mathrm{{Zn}(H t e a)\leftrightarrow\Delta_{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{{cam})}}$ to produce two enantiopure frameworks. It is not uncommon for organic racemic molecules to undergo spontaneous resolution during crystallization as is well-known in the discovery of chirality in sodium ammonium tartrate,65,118 such a phenomenon has been rarely observed during the synthesis of 3D MOFs. For example, in all earlier studies involving racemic DL-camphoric acid, both D- and
L-ligands are incorporated into the same structures, leading to racemic crystals.119,120
This work represents an interesting example that shows how multiple chiral features in MOFs interact with each other to produce new chemical, structural, and chiral phenomena. Furthermore, this process points to a new avenue for the resolution of racemates, which may have practical value in enantioseparation.
## 4.5 Homochiral metal–organic frameworks from achiral building blocks by chiral induction
Currently, there is an enormous demand for practical synthetic methods to allow the preparation of chiral materials.121–123 For the synthesis of enantiopure or enantioenriched molecules, a great progress has been achieved in the past several decades through the use of asymmetric homogeneous catalysts based on either chiral metal complexes or organocatalysts.124–128 In comparison, there have been very little progresses in the area of asymmetric crystallization from achiral precursors to form enantiopure or enantioenriched crystalline nanoporous materials. The latter is very important for the development of heterogeneous porous asymmetric catalysts based on crystalline inorganic or coordination compounds.
Zhang et al. explored the enantioselective effect of enantiopure organic acids and naturally occurring amino acids. The ultimate goal is to create a library of inexpensive chiral chemicals that can effect asymmetric crystallization of 3-D crystalline porous materials and to use these inexpensive asymmetric molecular catalysts such as organic acids and amino acids to create new high-valued crystalline heterogeneous asymmetric catalysts. With this strategy, three unprecedented MOFs were constructed: ${\bf{\Lambda}}_{\mathrm{{D}}^{-}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ $(\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{4}(\mathrm{adc}$ )) $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{adc}\ =\right.$ adamantane-1,3-dicarboxylic acid), $\mathbf{\tau}_{\mathrm{L-Mn}}$ , DL-Mn.129 The three MOFs are constituted by the 3D $\mathbf{Mn-O-Mn}$ framework of $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ with honeycomb channels along the $c$ axis. Decorative achiral organic chains of adc ligands line up the honeycomb channels by attaching to the wall of $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ through Mn-adc bonds (Fig. 19). It is worth noting that $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ is chiral and forms $3_{1}$ or $3_{2}$ helical structures along the $c$ axis. The most interesting aspect of its synthesis is the asymmetric crystallization catalyzed by molecular chiral additives. In addition, through a comparative study of four different crystallization processes with D-, L-, $\mathrm{DL}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cam}$ and without them, a much better understanding into the chirality induction mechanism was gained. The asymmetric crystallization of ${\bf{\Lambda}}_{\mathrm{{D}}-\mathbf{M}\mathrm{{n}}}$ and $\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\mathrm{L-Mn}}$ is believed to result from cooperative interactions between chiral $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ and achiral adc ligands during the nucleation of $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ frameworks. Because $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{cam}$ has similar coordination geometry with adc (in both, two –COO– groups are separated by three C atoms), camphorate ligand is capable of directly participating in the nucleation and crystallization process, which allows the control of the absolute helicity of $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ frameworks. On the other hand, because adc has a stronger binding ability to the $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{COO})_{4}]_{n}^{2n+}$ framework, adc ligands eventually replace the camphorate ligands in the final crystals, which is demonstrated by time-dependent experiments. The experiments show that the initial crystallization of an achiral phase $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{adc})$ which persists in the absence of a chiral induction agent, is slowly converted into enantioenriched $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{4}(\mathrm{adc})$ in the presence of a chiral induction agent such as D-camphoric acid, operating in synergy with in situ generated formate. The achiral to chiral conversion, together with concurrent control of handedness, results from cooperative interactions of three carboxylates. The discovery that camphoric acid can control the absolute chirality of the 3-D porous $\mathrm{[Mn_{3}(H C O O)_{4}]^{2+}}$ framework by either directly binding to the framework or by catalytic chiral induction (depending on whether the competing adc ligand is present or not) is truly unprecedented and extraordinary. This research develops a better understanding of chemical or structural factors that contribute to the asymmetric crystallization and direct the synthesis of asymmetric catalysts from achiral precursors.
Another example of a camphorate templated homochiral coordination polymer was reported by Suresh et al.130 Two enantiomeric pairs (1P, 1M and 2P, 2M) of isostructural twodimensional metal organic frameworks $\mathrm{[Zn(SO_{4})(b d b)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ and $\mathrm{[Co(SO_{4})(b d b)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ (where bdb $^{\circ}=1,4$ -bis(4-pyridyl)-2,3- diaza-1,3-butadiene) comprising achiral building blocks by the spatial orientation of the ligand moiety coordinated to the metal. Either of the $\mathbf{\Omega}_{1\mathrm{{P}/1M}}$ and $2\mathrm{P}/2\mathrm{M}$ pairs of chiral MOFs can be synthesized as a conglomerate by spontaneous resolution occurring upon diffusing the alcoholic solution of the ligand into the aqueous metal sulfate solution. They further show that the camphoric acid can act as a chirality inducing agent to achieve bulk absolute chiral CPs with the desired helicity (1P, 1M, 2P, and 2M). In the chirality induction experiments enantiopure camphoric acid can coerce the enantio-enrichment of bulk samples. Thus, MOFs 1P and 2P with absolute positive homohelicity have been obtained in the presence of $\mathbf{L}$ -cam whereas D-cam directs the exclusive formation of absolute negative homohelical CPs 1M and 2M. The individual crystals of 1P, 1M and 2P, 2M used for crystallographic studies were further characterized by solid-state circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy to confirm the absolute configurations, the CD spectra for 6 random crystals from both Zn and Co CPs crystallized in the presence of enantiopure camphoric acids and 10 crystals from each batch crystallized in the absence of camphoric acid were recorded using Nujol mull, which furnished complementary results to the crystallographic findings.
Absolute chiral crystallization of the isostructural Zn and Co coordination polymers has been achieved in the presence of enantiopure camphoric acids. This study highlights the feasibility of the induction process of enantiopure camphoric acids in systems exhibiting spontaneous resolution and hence opens up new avenues for the selective crystallization of desired enantiomorphs in MOFs/CPs comprising achiral ligands.
## 5 Applications of metal–camphorate frameworks
Recently, chiral MOFs have attracted much attention owing to their intriguing potential in the fields of enantioselective separation, asymmetric catalysis, nonlinear optics, and chiral sensors. As a unique subset of chiral MOFs, metal–camphorate frameworks are closely related to many interesting applications. Up to now, they are applied for lanthanide separation, enantiomer recognition and separation.
A large series of homochiral lanthanide camphorates frameworks $\mathrm{\small{[Ln(p\mathrm{-}c a m)(C H_{3}C O O)(H_{2}O)]}}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{D-cam})(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})\right]$ were reported by Bu et al.,79 which can be used to selectively crystallize or enrich specific lanthanide ions in predesigned MOFs. Bu et al. have demonstrated that crystallization processes by $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are very sensitive to ionic radii and that the ionic radius difference between two $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions dictates the unequal concentrations of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ in Ln-MOF crystals. Such preference by the MCamF structure for smaller $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ is shown by EDS results on various combinations of late lanthanides (from Tb to Yb and Y). Nine out of the 15 combinations show a separation factor higher than 1.25 (in other words, with an equimolar $\mathbf{M}2{-}\mathbf{M}2^{\prime}$ mixture, their distribution in the final crystal can reach about $55\mathrm{-}45\%$ in molar fraction) and four of them can reach over 1.5 ( $60\mathrm{-}40\%$ in molar fraction) (Fig. 20a). The result show that the selectivity is strongly dependent on the ionic radius difference between two $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, all 22 lanthanide combinations in this study are plotted with their selectivity versus ionic radius difference (Fig. 20b). As shown in Fig. 20c, the metal distributions in six Y–Ln combinations obtained from EDS measurements and crystallographic refinements are consistent with each other, with $<3\%$ difference (Fig. 20c). The work reported here shows the great potential for developing MOF platforms for other crystallization-based separation processes beyond just lanthanide mixtures.
Enantiomer adsorption and separation of chiral molecules is an important field of chemistry and has many important applications in pharmaceutical, agricultural and chemical engineering.131–133 So far, most studies have focused on powder MOFs obtained by hydro/solvothermal synthesis methods. For a number of applications, however, powder materials are not appropriate. The case of membrane and thin film fabrication of MOFs on the substrates exhibits striking advantages.134 In particular, surface anchored MOFs (SURMOFs) grown by the liquid-phase epitaxy (LPE) method afford the preparation of hetero-SURMOFs by changing the metal nodes and/or linker molecules in successive deposition cycles. The LPE approach is based on the sequential immersion of a SAM modified substrate into the solutions of metal salts and organic ligands. Between each step the substrates were rinsed with solvent to remove the uncoordinated metal nodes or organic linkers. The enantiopure metal–camphorate frameworks $\mathrm{Zn(n\mathrm{-cam)_{2}d a b c o}}$ and $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ are grown (20–40 cycles) by dipping the quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) substrate alternately in ethanol solutions of $\mathbf{Zn(Ac)_{2}\cdot H_{2}O}$ and equimolar (D or $\mathbf{\lambda_{L}})\mathbf{cam}/\mathbf{\lambda}$ dabco mixtures, each step followed by immediately rinsing with pure ethanol, according to the procedure developed in our group (Fig. 21a).135 The QCM is an ultrasensitive device capable of sensing mass changes in the nanogram range. The deposition of enantiopure directly on the QCM substrate enables the adsorption kinetics of enantiomeric probe molecules to be monitored by QCM and thus allow assessing the adsorption enantioselectivity of the MOF thin film. The results indicate that $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ displays a roughly 1.5-fold preference for adsorption of (2R,5R)-2,5-hexandiol $\left({\cal R\mathrm{-}H D O}\right)$ over (2S,5S)-2,5- hexanediol (S-HDO), whereas $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ exhibits the inverted selectivity, a 1.5-fold adsorption of $s$ -HDO over $R$ -HDO (Fig. 21b and c). This very significant difference is attributed to the different interaction of $R^{.}$ - and $s$ -HDO with MCamFs containing D- and L-forms of camphorate.
Using QCM, MCamFs also can be systematically investigated by the influence of the pore size of isoreticular homochiral MOFs, i.e. of MOFs with the same topology and identical chiral centers but with tunable pore sizes, on the enantiomer separation.
For this purpose, the enantioselectivity of the adsorption capacity of chiral probe molecules, $R^{-}$ and $s$ limonene, by the isoreticular MCamFs are studied (Fig. 22). This means a series of homochiral, pillared-layer $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{D}\cdot\mathrm{cam})_{2}(\mathrm{P})]$ were prepared with an identical chiral layer $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{p-cam})\right]$ and different pillar-linkers P: diazabicyclo[2.2.2]-octane (dabco), $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (bipy) and 1,4-bis(4-pyridyl)benzene (bipyb).136
The data show clearly that the pore size has a significant impact on the enantioselectivity. The enantioselectivity does not follow such a simple trend as the adsorption capacity, which increases with increasing pore size. The highest enantiomeric excess was found for a pore size of $0.8~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which was found to be the medium case. It can be assumed that the differences of the loadings are caused by the different alignments of the chiral guest molecules adsorbed in the pores, where the stereogenic centers have a different impact on the enantiomer selectivity.
For better visualization, Fig. 22 shows a sketch of (S)-limonene in the isoreticular homochiral MOFs. If the pore size is ‘‘too’’ small, the guest molecules are ‘‘forced’’ to adsorb in the pores in such a position, where the impact of the stereogenic center in the framework is small. If the pore size is ‘‘too’’ large, the molecules can adsorb all over the large pore and the impact of the stereogenic center is small, too. If the pore size is well adjusted, roughly as large as the guest molecule, the stereogenic center has the highest impact on the guest molecule, resulting in the highest enantiomer separation. This study demonstrates that not only the stereogenic center, but also the pore size have to be adjusted for gaining highest enantioselectivities in chiral nanoporous materials and thereupon enabling a significantly more efficient enantiomer separation.
Furthermore, the liquid phase epitaxial growth of MCamF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Dcam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ thin films enables the possibility to investigate the enantiomer separation from a racemic mixture by means of (oriented) circular dichroism (CD). The enantiomer excess of $(+)$ -ethyl-D-lactate $\scriptstyle{\big[}(+){\mathrm{EtLt}}{\big]}$ and $(-)$ -ethyl-L-lactate $\scriptstyle{[(-)\mathrm{EtLt}]}$ in $[\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{D}\mathbf{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ was investigated by CD (Fig. 23). By comparing the CD spectra of the pure EtLt and the enantiomer-pure EtLt adsorbed in the $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Dcam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ with the spectrum measured for the racemic EtLt mixture adsorbed in the $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Dcam})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})],$ an enantiomer excess (ee) (+) vs. () of 28% was found.137
The enantiomer separation property138 is studied by gas chromatography (GC) technology, which is a typical analytical method for separating and analyzing compounds without decomposition.139–141 In order to obtain a homogenous sample and perform the experiment of GC enantiomer separation well, the homochiral MCamF $[\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{Dcam})_{2}\mathbf{P}]$ $\mathbf{\tilde{P}}=\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}$ dabco and bipy) thin films were grown in the capillary column directly using liquid-phase epitaxy in a well-defined layer-by-layer fashion.
The morphology and cross-section of the MOF thin film in the column was checked by the SEM (Fig. 24a–d), showing that MOF $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{(\mathrm{D-cam})}_{2}\mathbf{P}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{P}}=\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}$ dabco and bipy) can be grown in the inner wall of the capillary column with compact and homogenous film using LPE layer by layer fashion successfully.
By comparing gas chromatography retention time (Fig. 24f) of probe molecules in a poly(L-DOPA) functionalized capillary column, $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{(\mathrm{D-cam})}_{2}\mathbf{P}$ grown in achiral hydroxyl and enantiopure poly(L-DOPA) functionalized capillary columns and $[\mathrm{Cu(\mathrm{D\cdotcam)P}]}$ grown in a poly(L-DOPA) functionalized column exhibited excellent separation efficiency for methyl lactate $\mathbf{\bar{P}}= $ dabco) and 1-phenylethanol $\mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{\lambda}}=\mathbf{\lambda} $ bipy), respectively. The explored chiral substrate mounted homochiral MOF thin film has high efficiency separation, which is effected by the chiral poly(L-DOPA) functionalized substrate.142 This work not only indicates that the LPE process might be a promising approach for introducing multiple chiral layers or films as stationary phases for highly effective separation, but also demonstrates that a chiral poly(L-DOPA) substrate has the ability to improve enantiomer separation for homochiral MOF thin films.
chiral and helical MOFs. Also, it is shown that it is possible to grow metal–camphorate frameworks with advanced features in different morphologies such as thin films with highly active surfaces, homogeneity, controllable thickness and orientation. Chiral thin films can enhance the applications for enantioseparation and asymmetric catalysis.
In terms of structural diversity, camphoric acid has perhaps produced more homochiral MOFs than any other chiral ligands and it has thus contributed greatly to our understanding of various chiral phenomena occurring in the solid state, MOFs in particular. It is expected that camphoric acids will continue to have a significant contribution in this field by playing a vital role in coordination chemistry and materials science.
## 5. Conclusions and outlook
## Abbreviations
In this review we show that camphoric acid, a low-cost chiral molecule readily available in enantiopure forms, exhibits a large variety of bonding behaviors by its carboxylate groups. Unlike other chiral ligands such as amino acids, camphoric acid is resistant to racemization, making it useable under a variety of synthetic conditions. Such chemical, structural and coordination characteristics make it an elegant building component for the construction of chiral frameworks, particularly those with enantioselectivity properties. The strategies for synthesizing porous homochiral MOFs with camphoric acid and different experimental methods developed so far are described. In the case of enantioselective applications using chiral metal– camphorate framework materials, large channels or cages need to be designed and constructed with the help of suitable auxiliary ligands, together with the use of enantiopure cam ligands as either building blocks or pendant decoration in the wall of channels or cages. Such exposed chirality centers are particularly desirable for chiral recognition and have considerable potential applications in the identification of chiral drugs or for drug delivery. In 2001, the Nobel Prize in chemistry was awarded to three chemists for the work in the production of chiral drugs with chiral catalysts. Nowadays, chiral synthesis for drug production bring huge economic and health benefits.
In addition to their potential enantioselective applications as mentioned in the text, one particular interest is that they can integrate both molecular chirality and absolute helicity, in a way that mimics the chiral and helical features of well-known biopolymers such as DNA. It is believed that the integrated chiral and helical features of MOFs may provide fresh opportunities and model systems for probing the relationship between chirality and helicity in biopolymers. Furthermore, it may be possible to imitate the method of DNA replication to synthesize
